### How does Hibernate dirty checking work?

Dirty checking is Hibernate's automatic change detection. When an entity is loaded into the persistence context, Hibernate takes a snapshot – a deep copy of all field values. At flush time (before commit), Hibernate compares every managed entity's current state against its snapshot. If any field changed, it generates an UPDATE SQL statement.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class AccountUpdateService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountUpdateService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void updateBalance(String accountId, BigDecimal newBalance) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        // Hibernate takes snapshot: {id=SAV2001, balance=500000, version=1}

        account.setBalance(newBalance);
        // No explicit save() needed!

        // At commit, Hibernate compares current vs snapshot:
        // current: {balance=400000} vs snapshot: {balance=500000}
        // Field changed! Generates:
        // UPDATE accounts SET balance=400000, version=2
        // WHERE account_id='SAV2001' AND version=1
    }
}
```

### What does @Transactional(readOnly=true) optimize?

When `readOnly=true`, Hibernate skips snapshot storage and dirty checking. For N loaded entities with M fields each, this saves N x M comparisons at flush time plus the memory for N snapshot copies.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dto.TradeOverview;
import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.TradeRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class TradeReportService {

    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;

    public TradeReportService(TradeRepository tradeRepo) {
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public List<TradeOverview> getSettledTrades(String accountId) {
        List<Trade> trades = tradeRepo.findByAccountIdAndStatus(
            accountId, "SETTLED");
        // Hibernate loads trades but does NOT take snapshots
        // At commit: NO dirty checking runs (no snapshots to compare)
        // Savings: no snapshot memory, no comparison CPU

        return trades.stream()
            .map(t -> new TradeOverview(t.getTradeId(), t.getSymbol(), t.getAmount()))
            .toList();
    }
}
```

```text
With readOnly=false (1000 entities, 20 fields each):
  Snapshot storage: 1000 copies x 20 fields = 20,000 field copies
  Flush comparison: 1000 entities x 20 fields = 20,000 comparisons
  Memory: ~5MB for snapshots

With readOnly=true:
  Snapshot storage: 0
  Flush comparison: 0
  Memory saved: ~5MB
```

### How does readOnly help at the database level?

The JDBC connection is also hinted as read-only. Some databases use this hint for optimizations.

- **PostgreSQL:** uses a lighter MVCC snapshot with reduced locking overhead
- **MySQL InnoDB:** skips undo log creation for read-only transactions
- **Connection pool:** read-only hint can route to a read replica in master-slave setups

```yaml
# application.yml: routing read-only queries to replica
spring:
  datasource:
    primary:
      url: jdbc:postgresql://primary:5432/national_bank
    replica:
      url: jdbc:postgresql://replica:5432/national_bank
  # @Transactional(readOnly=true) routes to replica datasource
  # @Transactional routes to primary datasource
```

### What is Open Session in View (OSIV)?

OSIV is a Spring Boot filter (`OpenEntityManagerInViewInterceptor`) that keeps the JPA EntityManager open for the entire HTTP request lifecycle – from controller entry through JSON serialization. It is enabled by default in Spring Boot.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
public class AccountController {

    private final AccountService accountService;

    public AccountController(AccountService accountService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    // WITH OSIV (default: spring.jpa.open-in-view=true):
    @GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
    public Account getAccount(@PathVariable String id) {
        Account account = accountService.findById(id);
        // @Transactional ends in service layer
        // But session is still OPEN because of OSIV!
        // JSON serialization triggers: account.getTrades()
        // Fires lazy load SQL. No LazyInitializationException.
        // BUT: DB connection held during entire serialization!
        return account;
    }
}
```

### Why is OSIV an anti-pattern in production?

OSIV causes three problems in production. First, database connections are held for the entire request duration including JSON serialization. Under load, the connection pool is exhausted. Second, lazy loads during serialization fire N+1 queries invisible to the service layer. Third, accidental writes during rendering could persist without an explicit @Transactional boundary.

```yaml
# WRONG: OSIV enabled (Spring Boot default)
spring:
  jpa:
    open-in-view: true
    # DB connection held from controller entry to response complete
    # Lazy loads fire during JSON serialization (hidden N+1)

# CORRECT: disable OSIV
spring:
  jpa:
    open-in-view: false
    # Spring Boot warns at startup if OSIV is enabled:
    # "spring.jpa.open-in-view is enabled by default.
    #  Therefore, database queries may be performed during view rendering."
```

### How do you fix LazyInitializationException without OSIV?

Fetch everything inside the `@Transactional` method using JOIN FETCH or `@EntityGraph`, then map to a DTO before returning. The DTO has no lazy proxies and is safe to serialize.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dto.AccountDetailDTO;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AccountService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // WRONG: returning entity to controller (lazy proxies explode without OSIV)
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Account findById(String id) {
        return accountRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    }

    // CORRECT: JOIN FETCH + map to DTO inside transaction
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public AccountDetailDTO getAccountDetail(String id) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findWithTrades(id).orElseThrow();
        // Account + trades loaded via JOIN FETCH in one query
        return new AccountDetailDTO(
            account.getAccountId(),
            account.getHolderName(),
            account.getBalance(),
            account.getTrades().stream()
                .map(t -> new AccountDetailDTO.TradeItem(
                    t.getTradeId(), t.getSymbol(), t.getAmount()))
                .toList()
        );
        // DTO has no proxies. Safe to serialize after session closes.
    }
}
```

### How is @Transactional(readOnly=true) different from @QueryHints(readOnly)?

`@Transactional(readOnly=true)` applies to the entire transaction – ALL queries skip dirty checking. `@QueryHints(readOnly=true)` applies to a single query – only entities loaded by that method skip dirty checking. Other queries in the same transaction still have full dirty checking.

| Scope | @Transactional(readOnly=true) | @QueryHints(readOnly=true) |
|-------|------------------------------|---------------------------|
| Applies to | Entire transaction | Single query |
| Dirty checking | Skipped for ALL entities | Skipped for THIS query's entities |
| JDBC hint | Connection hinted read-only | No connection hint |
| Use case | Read-only service methods | Mix of read and write in one TX |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.repository.TradeRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class PortfolioReportService {

    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public PortfolioReportService(TradeRepository tradeRepo,
                                   AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional  // NOT readOnly: this transaction writes
    public void generateAndStamp(String accountId) {
        // Trades loaded with @QueryHints(readOnly=true) on repo method
        var trades = tradeRepo.findByAccountId(accountId);
        // These Trade entities skip dirty checking (query-level readOnly)

        // Account loaded normally (with dirty checking)
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        account.setLastReportDate(java.time.LocalDate.now());
        // This Account IS dirty-checked. UPDATE generated at commit.
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with readOnly and OSIV?

The most common mistake is using `@Transactional(readOnly=true)` on a method that writes data. The write is silently ignored.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class ReadOnlyMistakeDemo {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public ReadOnlyMistakeDemo(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // WRONG: readOnly=true but method writes data
    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public void wrongDebit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        account.setBalance(account.getBalance().subtract(amount));
        // No snapshot taken, no dirty checking
        // Balance change is SILENTLY IGNORED!
    }

    // CORRECT: no readOnly for write operations
    @Transactional
    public void correctDebit(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        account.setBalance(account.getBalance().subtract(amount));
        // Dirty checking detects the change. UPDATE generated.
    }
}
```
